Part 2: Critical Quote Analysis
Quote 1 (p. 8)

“After all, it is a question of artistic feeling, and it is a great mistake to suppose that without this you can produce artistic work.”

In Andrews’s framework, artistic feeling refers to the ability to recognize beauty, atmosphere, mood, and expressive qualities within nature. It involves more than technical knowledge or mechanical skill. A person may understand cameras and photographic chemicals, yet still fail to create meaningful art if they lack artistic sensitivity. Andrews also stresses that artistic feeling should be refined through education and through studying nature and great artists.

This internal sensibility transforms a mechanical routine into an artistic process because it guides every creative decision. The artist chooses subjects carefully, studies changing light conditions, and waits for the ideal moment rather than accepting the first available image. Artistic feeling lets the photographer use the machine as a tool for expression rather than letting it dictate the result.

Quote 2 (p. 12)

“Emerson has well expressed it when he defined art as ‘Nature passed through the alembic of man.’”

The alembic metaphor suggests a process of transformation. According to Emerson and Andrews, art is not simply nature copied exactly as it appears. Instead, nature enters the artist’s mind and is transformed through imagination, emotion, experience, and judgment. The final artwork reflects both the external world and the artist's unique perspective.

If nature is the raw material, the “alembic of man” represents the filtering process of human consciousness. The artist selects important details, emphasizes mood, arranges composition, and communicates feelings that would otherwise remain hidden. The resulting artwork is therefore more than a factual record. It is nature interpreted through a human intelligence capable of finding meaning, beauty, and emotional significance within the world.

Quote 3 (p. 12)

“Photography is a most useful and curious invention and of the greatest value for the record of plain facts about persons and places, but it is not a fine art at all and can never be made one.”

Hamerton argues that photography cannot become fine art because it records appearances rather than transforming them. In his view, photography captures facts mechanically, whereas fine art requires conscious selection, imagination, and idealization. A camera reproduces details faithfully but does not interpret or elevate them independently.

Under Hamerton’s standards, a medium qualifies as fine art only when it allows meaningful creative transformation. Fine art must communicate more than factual accuracy. It must reveal an artist’s unique vision and interpretation of reality.

Emerson’s alembic metaphor challenges Hamerton’s criticism. If a photographer carefully composes a scene, chooses lighting, and intentionally guides the process, then nature still passes through human consciousness before becoming a photograph. Hamerton would likely argue that the camera limits this transformation and remains primarily factual, while Emerson and Andrews would emphasize the human choices that shape the final image.

Part 3: The Family of the Arts and the Contemporary AI Controversy
The Kinship of Media

Following Andrews’s reasoning, I would classify digital art and traditional painting as sisters within the same artistic family. Both depend on human intention, composition, and creative decision-making, even though they use different tools. Generative AI occupies a more complicated position. It is neither entirely separate from art nor fully equivalent to traditional artistic practices. Instead, AI functions as a new technological branch within the artistic family.

From Emerson’s perspective, AI-generated imagery may qualify as art when it reflects meaningful human input. If an artist develops concepts, crafts prompts, evaluates outputs, and selects successful results, then the final image has passed through the “alembic of man.” However, Hamerton would likely reject AI art because the machine performs much of the image creation. He would argue that AI primarily generates statistical combinations rather than expressing genuine artistic imagination.

Evaluating the Colorado State Fair Verdict

I believe it was fair for Jason M. Allen to win the competition because he disclosed that he used Midjourney and followed the competition’s rules. Creating the final image involved writing prompts, refining ideas, selecting among multiple results, and enhancing the chosen work. Although AI generated much of the visual content, human judgment remained essential throughout the process.

From Hamerton’s perspective, AI-generated work would fail to qualify as fine art because the machine performs the actual image construction. He would likely see it as a sophisticated method of recording or assembling information rather than genuine artistic creation.

From Andrews’s and Emerson’s perspectives, the artistic value depends on the human guidance involved. The prompts represent intentional choices, while selection and refinement demonstrate artistic feeling. In this view, AI is not replacing the artist but serving as a new tool, much like the camera did in the nineteenth century.

My conclusion is that generative AI can support authentic artistic practice when used thoughtfully and creatively. The technology itself is not artistic, but the human decisions behind its use can be. Like photography in Andrews’s era, AI challenges traditional definitions of art, yet artistic value continues to depend on imagination, judgment, and creative intention rather than the tool alone.
